# Failed-login limits per IP and per account (server/README.md "Failed-login limits", docs/SECURITY_CHECKLIST.md).
# The stock engine lets anyone try passwords as fast as it can reconnect: its throttle (Ban::acceptConnection) only stops
# 6 connections within 500 ms, and nothing counts wrong passwords. The website limits its own logins; the game's
# account login (7171) and game login (7172) had no limit, and each wrong guess costs a PBKDF2 run on the login worker.
#   * src/loginlimit.h (new, header only, no engine types; server/tools/loginlimit_test.cpp tests it): after
#     loginFailMaxPerIp (20) failed logins from one IP, or loginFailMaxPerAccount (50) on one account name from any IP,
#     within loginFailWindow (600) seconds, that IP / account is refused for loginFailBlock (900) seconds. 0 turns a
#     limit off. The account name is compared case-insensitively. A good login clears the account's count, never the
#     IP's (an attacker's own account cannot reset it). The maps are bounded (100,000 entries each, expired ones swept).
#   * ProtocolLogin / ProtocolGame: the check runs before the password check is queued (no PBKDF2 for a blocked IP or
#     account) and refuses with "Too many failed logins. Please wait N minutes ...". The result of each check is
#     recorded on the dispatcher. One console line when a block starts: [Login limit] ip|account blocked ...
#   * behind the gateways the IP is the player's own (patch 0025). The website's own login limit is separate.
# Apply in the server source root after 0001-0046: patch -p1 < 0047-login-failure-limit.patch, then rebuild.
